    </exercise>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-successive-transformations">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-order-of-two-reflections">
            <statement><p>Find the single matrix, and name the single transformation, for each of these.
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>The reflection in the line <m>y = x</m> followed by the reflection in the line <m>y = -x</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The reflection in the line <m>y = -x</m> followed by the reflection in the line <m>y = x</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The reflection in the <m>x</m>-axis followed by the reflection in the line <m>y = x</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The reflection in the line <m>y = x</m> followed by the reflection in the <m>x</m>-axis.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, rotation of <m>180^{\circ}</m> about <m>O</m> (b) the same matrix and rotation: these two orders happen to agree (c) <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, rotation of <m>90^{\circ}</m> anticlockwise about <m>O</m> (d) <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, rotation of <m>90^{\circ}</m> clockwise about <m>O</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>(c) The reflection in the <m>x</m>-axis is done first, so it goes on the right: <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>. (d) <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Two reflections always give a rotation; the order decides its direction.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-single-matrix-and-image">
            <statement><p><m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>B = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Find the single matrix for <q><m>A</m> followed by <m>B</m></q>, and the final image of the point <m>(1, -1)</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>BA = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ -2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>; <m>(1, -3)</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-three-transformations">
            <statement><p>A shape is rotated <m>90^{\circ}</m> anticlockwise about <m>O</m>, then rotated <m>90^{\circ}</m> anticlockwise about <m>O</m> again, and then reflected in the <m>x</m>-axis. Find the single matrix and name the single transformation.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>: the reflection in the <m>y</m>-axis</p></answer>
            <solution><p>Two quarter turns make a half turn, <m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>. The reflection is done last, so it goes on the left: <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, which sends <m>(1, 0)</m> to <m>(-1, 0)</m> and leaves <m>(0, 1)</m> fixed: the reflection in the <m>y</m>-axis.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-single-matrix-from-final-image">
            <statement><p>After two transformations, <m>(1, 1)</m> ends at <m>(4, 2)</m> and <m>(1, -1)</m> ends at <m>(-2, 4)</m>. Find the single matrix for the two transformations together.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 3 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Let the matrix be <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}</m>. Then <m>a + b = 4</m> and <m>a - b = -2</m>, so <m>a = 1</m>, <m>b = 3</m>; and <m>c + d = 2</m>, <m>c - d = 4</m>, so <m>c = 3</m>, <m>d = -1</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-turning-a-signboard">
            <statement><p>A design for a shop signboard in Machakos is rotated <m>90^{\circ}</m> anticlockwise about <m>O</m> and then enlarged, centre <m>O</m>, scale factor <m>3</m>. Find the single matrix, and the image of the corner <m>(4, 1)</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -3 \\ 3 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>; <m>(-3, 12)</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>The enlargement is done second, so it goes on the left: <m>\begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -3 \\ 3 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Then <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -3 \\ 3 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 12 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-when-order-does-not-matter">
            <title>Challenge: When the Order Does Not Matter</title>
            <statement><p>Show that the rotation of <m>180^{\circ}</m> about <m>O</m> followed by any transformation with matrix <m>M</m> has the same effect as <m>M</m> followed by the rotation of <m>180^{\circ}</m>.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>The rotation of <m>180^{\circ}</m> has matrix <m>-I</m>. Work out <m>M(-I)</m> and <m>(-I)M</m>.</p></hint>
            <answer><p><m>M(-I) = -M = (-I)M</m>, so the two single matrices are equal.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>Let <m>M = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}</m>. The rotation first, then <m>M</m>, has matrix <m>M\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -a \amp -b \\ -c \amp -d \end{bmatrix}</m>. <m>M</m> first, then the rotation, has matrix <m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}M = \begin{bmatrix} -a \amp -b \\ -c \amp -d \end{bmatrix}</m>. The two single matrices are the same, so the order does not matter here.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-successive-transformations">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Focus on activity parts (a) to (c). Give a step table with columns <q>object</q>, <q>after the first</q>, <q>after the second</q>, and a card: <q>first matrix on the right</q>. Work through <xref ref="example-enlarge-then-reflect"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-order-of-two-reflections"/> and <xref ref="ex-single-matrix-and-image"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity plus all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-order-of-two-reflections"/> to <xref ref="ex-turning-a-signboard"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-when-order-does-not-matter"/>. Extension: find two different transformations from the table of standard matrices whose combination, in either order, is the rotation of <m>180^{\circ}</m> about <m>O</m>. (Answer: for example the reflections in the <m>x</m>-axis and the <m>y</m>-axis, or the reflections in <m>y = x</m> and <m>y = -x</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

